WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.139 --> 00:00:06.979
There are some remarkable cities dotted around
the world but what about the ones that have

00:00:06.979 --> 00:00:09.389
been consigned to the past?

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:15.410
Today we look at 9 amazing cities that were
once bustling hubs, full of life but are now

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:22.869
a mere shell of their former selves, forever
lost to history.

00:00:22.869 --> 00:00:30.779
Our first lost city is in south-western USA
and it was probably abandoned by the 14th

00:00:30.779 --> 00:00:31.779
century.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:34.870
White immigrants rediscovered it in the late
1800s.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:40.649
The local Native American’s obviously knew
of its existence but left these sacred ancestral

00:00:40.649 --> 00:00:42.800
sites uninhabited.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:51.120
Mesa Verde in Colorado is a large national
park and it holds around 4,300 ancient dwellings,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.109
with the largest being Cliff Palace.

00:00:53.109 --> 00:00:59.559
This giant 150 room structure follows the
shallow curve of an overhanging cliff and,

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.539
with its sandstone wall, looks like it’s
been carved right out of the rock.

00:01:02.539 --> 00:01:07.860
And if you’re thinking, wait; that’s not
a city, remember this was built after a long

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:09.860
history of nomadic travelling.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:13.640
The people would constantly be on the move,
following the seasons and the various animals

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.000
that they hunted.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.930
So to build something this big and this permanent
was impressive.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.390
It would be just like you coming from a tiny
farming village and getting off the train

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.460
in New York.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.810
Historians call the people Puebloans, from
the Spanish “pueblo” meaning town.

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:36.740
They started building permanent structures
in around 750 AD, as a way to store food for

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:40.869
longer, probably to get some down time when
they were sick of running after buffalo.

00:01:40.869 --> 00:01:45.500
As settlements grew, they increased their
reliance on farming since they could no longer

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.280
move with the migrating animals.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:52.010
Plus, the more they built, the more wood they
needed and so the less forest there was for

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.229
them to forage and hunt in.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:56.490
So what happened to its inhabitants?

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:01.969
Well, unlike many great Native American peoples,
it wasn’t the early settlers who drove them

00:02:01.969 --> 00:02:03.549
out of their homes.

00:02:03.549 --> 00:02:08.530
More likely it was a changing climate and
a series of mega-droughts in the 13th century

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.759
which hindered their farming.

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:15.840
No matter how cool your house is, you’re
not going to stay if you can’t eat.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:21.120
We’re heading around the other side of the
world now, to Ancient Mesopotamia, which is

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.880
considered by many to be the cradle of civilization.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.270
With creations such as writing, metallurgy
and farming, they started the long journey

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:36.260
that led to you being able play Angry Birds,
on your smartphone, whilst eating a Big Mac.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:40.940
And Mesopotamia housed one of mankind’s
greatest cities; Babylon.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:50.030
At its peak, it had a population of over 200,000
people, which was around 0.003% of the world’s

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:56.370
population, or to put that in modern terms,
it would be over 20 million people.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:03:00.100
Only Tokyo is bigger than that right now.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:05.049
The city has a long and violent story and
empires have risen and fallen around it.

00:03:05.049 --> 00:03:11.620
It appeared in history around 2300 BC, although
who built it and exactly when are a little

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:15.900
fuzzy, since 3000 years of bad filing have
meant it’s kind of difficult to find the

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:17.440
original planning application.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:24.230
After 2000 BC the rise of the Babylonian empire
began and the city became the key holy place

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:29.140
of Mesopotamia and was likely the largest
city in the world at that point.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:33.530
It went through a bit of a slump, but hey,
we all do, and then grew again to greatness

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.360
between 600 – 300 BC.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:42.120
It’s been ruled by such leaders as Nebuchadnezzar,
who created the famous Hanging Gardens of

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.760
Babylon, and by Alexander the Great.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:49.200
When Alexander died, the battle to take over
his power basically emptied the city.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.780
I can only imagine his wake was full of big
egos and an awful lot of wine.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:59.799
It was also supposedly the home of the mythical
Tower of Babel, which was written about in

00:03:59.799 --> 00:04:01.310
the book of Genesis.

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:05.370
The tower was built to reach all the way up
into the heavens and the architects hoped

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:10.129
it would act as a sign that their people would
remain and not be spread out across the world.

00:04:10.129 --> 00:04:14.890
But then God came down and threw a spanner
in the works by making everyone speak a different

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:19.040
language, so they had to wander off to separate
corners of the earth.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.420
You could read this all as an allegory, showing
that Mesopotamia really was the birthplace

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:26.850
of all civilizations.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.320
So where is Babylon now?

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.330
Its ruins lie in modern day Iraq.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:36.950
In the 1980s, Saddam Hussein started to rebuild
parts of it to demonstrate the great history

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:38.730
of the Arab region.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:44.170
He also decided to add a huge portrait of
himself and Nebuchadnezzar and inscribed “this

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:49.070
was built by Saddam Hussein, son of Nebuchadnezzar,
to glorify Iraq”.

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:54.490
If you’re going to be a twisted, evil dictator,
you may as well do it with style.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:05:00.480
Now we travel south, across the Mediterranean
to reach Carthage, a lost city on the tip

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.810
of Tunisia, at Africa’s point closest to
Italy.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:09.550
The area is now a suburb of the Tunisian capital;
Tunis, but it was once the great power hub

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.450
of the area, the base of a naval empire.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:19.290
The city was created by Phoenician colonists
in about 814 BC, travelling over from their

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:22.050
home, in what is now known as Lebanon.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:27.280
They were led by their queen, Dido - no, not
the late 90s British pop artist.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:33.660
Queen Dido was worshipped for many years after
her death, this was largely due to her flamboyant

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:34.660
end.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:40.480
A rival king was threatening to invade Carthage
if she didn’t consent to marry him.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:45.180
She built a huge pyre, pretending that she
was burning everything that reminded her of

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.760
her previous, now deceased, husband.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.370
But at the final moment she leapt onto the
fire and fell on her own sword.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:58.280
Now that’s what I call a rejection; it makes
someone not showing up to a date seem rather

00:05:58.280 --> 00:05:59.280
mild in comparison.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.760
“Do you want to go out with me?”

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:04.020
“I’d rather fall on my own sword, whilst
simultaneously enveloped in flames.”

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.400
“Think I’ll take that as a no then.”

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:13.110
Carthage was actually very difficult to attack
though, with its massive navy of 220 warships

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:16.900
and an enormous 37 kilometres of wall surrounding
it.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:21.580
In fact, it wasn’t until 146 BC that it
first fell.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:25.451
The Romans, who were in a pretty bad mood
from dealing with Hannibal (the Carthaginian

00:06:25.451 --> 00:06:29.710
military commander, not the cannibal) for
15 years, took the city.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:35.860
They then burnt the ships and sold some 50,000
Carthaginians into slavery.

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:39.820
The Romans then built it up into one of the
largest cities in their empire.

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:46.340
The Vandals took it over for a while but eventually,
in 698 AD there was the second great battle

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:47.460
of Carthage.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:54.140
The armies of Umayyad, one of the large Islamic
Caliphates, completely destroyed the city,

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:58.060
cutting off its water, tearing down the walls
and wrecking the harbour.

00:06:58.060 --> 00:07:00.250
It really ruined its retail value.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:06.110
Our next city was left half-abandoned in the
jungle for around 300 years but has now become

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:08.650
one of the world’s largest tourist attractions.

00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:14.850
Angkor Wat is the largest religious monument
in the world and is the pride of Cambodia,

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.390
even featuring on their national flag.

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:22.690
Constructed in the 12th century by King Suryavarman
II, it was originally designed as a Hindu

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:26.330
temple but had become Buddhist by the end
of the century.

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.510
This was mainly due to a change in the beliefs
of the land.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:35.530
The beautiful temples we see now are only
a small part of what used to exist though.

00:07:35.530 --> 00:07:41.990
Angkor was in fact the largest pre-industrial
city in the world and between 1010 and 1220

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:49.230
it held at least 0.1% of the world’s population
within its 1000 square kilometre sprawl.

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:54.460
There are a number of theories as to why this
architectural marvel ended up mostly deserted.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:08:00.040
There was an uprising against a ruler from
Siam in 1431, since the Siamese had taken

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.120
control of the city some 80 years earlier.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:06.680
This probably led a large portion of people
to migrate out of the city.

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:12.380
But it could also have been a mixture of floods,
earthquakes and the bubonic plague; hopefully

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:15.400
not all at once, that would have been a really
bad week.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:20.160
So Angkor was left to rot in the jungle and
some monks stayed on in the temple, which

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:22.960
is most of what remains of the city today.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:28.490
The large moat, that surrounds the holy building,
mostly protected it from the encroaching vegetation

00:08:28.490 --> 00:08:31.880
and from the bullets and shells of the Cambodian
civil war.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:37.130
Ironically, despite all the conservation work,
it’s now under more threat than ever since

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:42.360
all of those tourists marching over the sandstone
and using up the ground water are weakening

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:44.770
the buildings day by day.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:49.690
The damage of 500 years of jungle growth is
nothing compared to what a little kid with

00:08:49.690 --> 00:08:52.540
a football and an ice cream can do.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:56.330
Okay, let’s come out of the jungle and head
to higher ground.

00:08:56.330 --> 00:09:02.250
Machu Picchu sits almost two and a half kilometres
above sea level and had a brief but mysterious

00:09:02.250 --> 00:09:03.250
history.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:08.250
The Incas built it in 1450 but we are still
unclear as to its purpose.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:13.370
Since the site, although very beautiful, has
no real strategic benefit in terms of trade

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.470
or food or power.

00:09:15.470 --> 00:09:19.140
Perhaps it was just a king trying to be difficult,
you know how kings are.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:23.440
The most likely reason is that it was some
sort of royal retreat, like the US president’s

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:28.270
Camp David, or Hitler’s Eagle’s Nest up
in the mountains of Bavaria.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:33.570
No eagles for the Incas though, they had the
large stone Altar of the Condor where they

00:09:33.570 --> 00:09:38.800
would perform human sacrifices and the birds
would come and remove the carcasses.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:44.270
The Incas were quite advanced and the construction
of some of Machu Picchu’s temples is so

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:49.470
accurate that you can’t even fit a sheet
of paper between the stonework, meaning it

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:51.830
holds together without mortar.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:56.010
The stepped terraces of the city are also
a very smart way of adapting the mountain

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:57.670
to the needs of the city.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:03.060
Surprisingly, their civilisation never got
around to inventing the wheel, although perhaps

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:07.450
if you’re over 2 kilometres up a hill, the
last thing you want is to see your cart rolling

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.660
off down into the nearest valley.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:16.340
In just over 100 years, the city was deserted,
possibly due to the devastation caused by

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:21.240
smallpox, which the Conquistadors brought
with them from Spain; what a lovely welcome

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:22.590
gift.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:28.710
On to Italy now and into a bay on the west
coast that also holds the city of Naples.

00:10:28.710 --> 00:10:34.760
Naples has existed in some form for around
3000 years, making it one of the longest continuously

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:36.280
inhabited places on earth.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:42.820
But across the other side of the bay lies
Pompeii, and Pompeii was not quite so lucky.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:49.130
In 79 AD, the entire Roman city was destroyed
and buried under a huge mound of ash and pumice

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:56.150
up to six metres deep, killing some 16,000
people, when Mount Vesuvius erupted.

00:10:56.150 --> 00:11:01.930
A mixture of ash, stones and gases were blown
33 kilometres into the air and an astonishing

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:09.320
1.5 million tonnes of pumice and molten rock
were spat out of the volcano every second!

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:15.380
This ended the 500-year history of Pompeii,
having been built at some point around 700-600

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:16.460
BC.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:22.200
So, I guess some of you are thinking; why
build your city next to a giant volcano?

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.350
That just seems like trouble.

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:29.880
Well, it was actually a pretty quiet volcano
then, since the previous eruption had been

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.900
a relatively small one almost 200 years before.

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:38.860
Still, 16 years before the eruption that destroyed
Pompeii, there was a huge earthquake that

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:42.770
took out a lot of the city and led to looting
and chaos.

00:11:42.770 --> 00:11:48.430
This was less a red flag, more an entire flag
factory making 10 metres square banners saying

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:51.310
“move away from the giant fire bucket”.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:56.650
Excavation of the city began in 1738 when
workmen discovered it whilst digging the foundations

00:11:56.650 --> 00:11:57.990
for a summer palace.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:02.490
It’s a huge tourist attraction with much
of the art and buildings restored.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.490
Vesuvius hasn’t erupted since 1944.

00:12:05.490 --> 00:12:13.160
But with 30 separate eruptions since 79 AD,
this vivacious volcano shows no signs of stopping

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:20.370
and poses an enormous threat to the 3 million
people who now live within 20 miles of its

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:21.370
crater.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.920
For our next city we travel to the deserts
of Jordan.

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:29.350
Petra was a city carved into the walls of
looming sandstone, which form a natural fortress

00:12:29.350 --> 00:12:30.350
around it.

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:35.980
It lay on the key caravan route through to
Gaza and the Red Sea so it was the centre

00:12:35.980 --> 00:12:37.630
of Nabataen trade.

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:41.690
The Nabateans were described as “one of
the most gifted people of the ancient world”

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.540
and it’s hard to argue with.

00:12:43.540 --> 00:12:49.230
The Petra we see now, of stunning tombs cut
with beautiful detail, and ingenious waterways

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:53.779
that made an arid land thrive, was probably
built around the 1st century.

00:12:53.779 --> 00:12:58.590
At the same time in ancient Britain, we were
pretty pleased that the romans had invaded

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:01.830
so we didn’t have to live in a circle of
mud and sticks anymore.

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:07.130
The tombs and temples are often much smaller
inside than their grand entrances would suggest

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:13.090
but, despite this, experts believe that we
have only uncovered around 15% of what is

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:14.090
there.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:16.420
There are many secrets yet to be found.

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:21.480
Some Bedouin tribes believe there is treasure
in the rocks and you can see the bullet holes

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.940
where they have tried to shoot it out.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:29.290
The Romans came along in 106 AD and the city
began to empty as its importance as a trade

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:33.690
route decreased and by 700 AD it was consigned
to history.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:38.700
If you don’t want to make the long journey
to see the ancient city, you’ll be glad

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:43.030
to know it’s on Google street view so you
can enjoy some of its wonders without even

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:44.640
having to get dressed.

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:50.529
Our final two cities have more of an air of
mystery around them since we have no definite

00:13:50.529 --> 00:13:56.620
proof that they actually existed, nor do we
know exactly where they could have been.

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.400
Our first legend is the magical city of gold;
El Dorado

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:05.779
In the earlier versions of the story, El Dorado
was a man and may have been the king of the

00:14:05.779 --> 00:14:10.980
native Muisca people, a tribe from near modern
day Bogota in Columbia.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:15.240
When a new king was appointed, he would be
covered in gold dust and then dipped into

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:20.370
the waters of Lake Guatavita while his onlookers
also threw in gold jewellery.

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:24.980
From the mid 16th century and for hundreds
of years after, many attempts to find the

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:28.110
city were made but nothing was ever found.

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:32.650
A lot of the myth was fuelled by the fact
that the conquistadors saw many of the local

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:38.080
people wearing gold, without seeming to understand
its value, so they assumed there must be some

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.270
huge stash of it somewhere.

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:45.560
One failed attempt resulted in the discovery
of a great river and when a tribe of warrior

00:14:45.560 --> 00:14:50.740
women chased the explorers off, their similarity
to a Greek myth was noted and the river was

00:14:50.740 --> 00:14:51.839
named; the Amazon.

00:14:51.839 --> 00:14:58.260
The English explorer, Sir Walter Raleigh led
two great expeditions to Venezuela, aiming

00:14:58.260 --> 00:15:04.710
to find El Dorado and to establish an English
colony in South America and disrupt the Spanish

00:15:04.710 --> 00:15:06.830
trade with the native people.

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:11.680
On the second journey he was on strict orders
not to get into any battles with the Spanish

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:14.670
due to the delicate politics back home.

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:19.480
Raleigh stayed on the island of Trinidad but
his son was killed in an inevitable fight

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:20.910
with the Spanish.

00:15:20.910 --> 00:15:24.390
When Raleigh returned home, he was beheaded
for disobeying orders.

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:29.500
I’m not sure what they really expected though,
fighting the Spanish was pretty much a national

00:15:29.500 --> 00:15:31.710
pastime around that time.

00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:38.070
Since the city El Dorado proved impossible
to find, many resorted to searching Lake Guatavita.

00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:43.100
There were many attempts but surely the best
was when a London company drained the whole

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:48.910
lake in 1898 and were annoyed to find that
the remaining mud baked as hard as concrete

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:52.760
in the jungle sun, making it almost unpassable.

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:57.570
They uncovered a pathetic £500 worth of gold
for their efforts.

00:15:57.570 --> 00:16:02.960
They probably made more money in free drinks
when they told the story in the pub back home.

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:08.010
And finally, the most famous lost city of
them all; Atlantis.

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:13.310
The legend of an island lost to the sea has
kept going for thousands of years but no evidence

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:18.589
has ever been found and there is a general
consensus amongst historians that it has no

00:16:18.589 --> 00:16:20.940
real basis in truth.

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:26.190
Its origin is largely down to the Greek philosopher
Plato who discussed an imaginary island as

00:16:26.190 --> 00:16:31.500
part of his work The Republic which he used
to explore the idea of a perfect state.

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:36.970
In the story, Ancient Athenians are the only
people able to defend against Atlantean attack

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:42.380
and the island itself loses favour with the
gods who decided to submerge it into the Atlantic

00:16:42.380 --> 00:16:43.870
Ocean.

00:16:43.870 --> 00:16:48.899
Probably a bit harsh but then Greek gods aren’t
exactly famous for their calm demeanour and

00:16:48.899 --> 00:16:53.970
balanced decision making, they were far too
engrossed with drinking, incest and making

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:55.990
animals with lots of heads.

00:16:55.990 --> 00:17:00.940
Plato’s story stuck though and there have
been hundreds of proposed sites for this lost

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:07.010
city; everywhere from the Mediterranean to
the Antarctica and even the Bermuda Triangle.

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:12.059
The people of Atlantis were rumoured to be
a technologically advanced super race with

00:17:12.059 --> 00:17:13.640
submarines and aircraft.

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:18.440
But if they were so smart, why didn’t they
do something when they noticed the sea levels

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:20.000
were rising around them.

00:17:20.000 --> 00:18:03.590
Or maybe they did and we just haven’t found
them yet.

